# ============================================================
# vivera - .graphifyignore
# Created: 2026-10-01
# ============================================================
#
# HOW TO READ THIS FILE
#
# graphify (CLI: ~/.local/bin/graphify, v0.9.73) reads this file using
# .gitignore syntax. Its patterns are merged with .gitignore, and are
# evaluated LAST, so where the two disagree this file wins.
#
# Scope note: unlike the Obsidian Vault, this repository has no
# credential-bearing directories and no restricted scopes. These entries
# exist to keep generated, vendored, and VCS-internal files out of the
# knowledge graph. There is no cloud-extraction boundary declared here
# yet — if one is added, revisit this file before the first cloud run.
#
# Facts below were checked on 2026-10-01 against this working copy.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUP 1 — TOOL BUILD OUTPUT
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# graphify's own output directory. Ingesting it would feed generated
# artifacts back into the graph. AGENTS.md at the repo root describes
# this project's graph as living in graphify-out/, but the directory does
# not exist in the working copy as of 2026-10-01. The pattern is kept so
# it holds as soon as the graph is first built.
graphify-out/

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUP 2 — INSTALLED DEPENDENCIES
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored third-party packages, not project source. Present in the
# working copy. Pulling these in would add tens of thousands of
# third-party files to the graph and drown out project code.
node_modules/

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUP 3 — VCS INTERNALS
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# Object store and refs; not project source. Present in the working copy.
.git/

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUP 4 — SPIKE / THROWAWAY OUTPUT
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# spike-out/ holds output from throwaway spike runs rather than
# maintained project artifacts, so it is excluded to keep the graph
# describing the real repository. Present in the working copy.
spike-out/

# ------------------------------------------------------------
# GROUP 5 — CLOUD EXTRACTION BOUNDARY (added 2026-10-02)
# ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY THIS GROUP EXISTS
#
# The scope note at the top of this file was written 2026-10-01 and states
# plainly that no cloud-extraction boundary is declared here yet, and that this
# file must be revisited before the first cloud run. This group is that revisit.
# The earlier note is left byte-for-byte unchanged so that the diff of this
# addition shows added lines only; where the two disagree, this group is the
# later and correct statement.
#
# The measured fact, from docs/graphify-integration.md §1 and §6.4: markdown and
# other document extraction REQUIRES an LLM backend, there is no local-only mode
# for .md, and the only viable backend on this machine is a cloud model
# (gpt-oss:120b-cloud via Ollama). A full `graphify extract` run here therefore
# submits this repository's document content to a cloud provider. There is no
# local model to fall back to; the local ones were measured unusable (§5).
#
# TWO CASES, NOT ONE
#
# 1. CODE. Symbols come from local AST parsing, which needs no LLM and no key, so
#    a code-only corpus contributes no document text to any request.
#    `graphify update` is measured AST-only with no API cost (§6.6).
# 2. DOCS / PAPERS / IMAGES. These need semantic extraction, so their content is
#    chunked and transmitted. `--code-only` skips doc/paper/image files (§3.1,
#    §6.4), which is what keeps that content out of the corpus. UNMEASURED:
#    whether a code-only `extract` still submits code-derived text to obtain
#    semantic edges. Until that is measured, read `--code-only` as "docs stay
#    home", not as "nothing leaves this machine".
#
# CONSEQUENCE AND REMEDY
#
# Do not run a full `graphify extract` in this repository until you have decided
# that sending this repository's documentation to a provider is acceptable.
# `--code-only` is the option that keeps documents local. Pointing `--backend` at
# a different provider, for example one reached with a Gemini API key, changes WHO
# receives the corpus, not WHETHER it is transmitted. graphify has no
# `--no-graphifyignore` flag (§4.1, §6.5), so this file is the only control over
# what the backend is allowed to accept: an over-broad pattern here is a data
# exfiltration bug and not a slow build (§4.2).
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT A FIREWALL
#
# It cannot prevent egress. It decides what ENTERS the corpus, and the ignore
# files are the only boundary that exists. What is written below is a decision
# record plus an exclusion list, and it holds only while it is read before a run
# rather than after one.
#
# CREDENTIAL SCAN — 2026-10-02, NOTHING FOUND
#
# The working copy was searched, excluding .git/ and node_modules/, for *.env,
# .env*, *credential*, *secret*, *.key, *.pem, *token* and *.bak* filenames and
# for API-key-shaped string values. Zero real hits. Two files match a naive
# secret scan and are false positives: scripts/lib/redact.test.mjs and
# scripts/lib/redaction-selftest.test.mjs contain synthetic strings such as
# "api_key=synthetic-fixture-value-1" that exercise the redaction helpers. They
# are not secrets and are deliberately not listed here.
# This repository has no credential-bearing directory, so none is named below.
# A boundary naming paths that do not exist is worse than no boundary. Re-run the
# scan and add any credential-bearing path HERE, before the first cloud run.
#
# The paths below must never enter a cloud extraction corpus. Each was verified
# present in this working copy on 2026-10-02 with `test -e`.
#
# All of them are already excluded above (GROUP 1 to GROUP 4). They are repeated
# here because under this boundary each is load-bearing for a second and
# different reason. Patterns in this file are last-match-wins, so repeating them
# changes nothing about the result; the duplication is deliberate documentation.
#
# graphify-out/  Now exists — it did not on 2026-10-01, which is what GROUP 1's
#                comment records, and that comment is left unchanged. Beyond
#                being build output, this directory holds
#                .graphify_chunk_01..03.json and .graphify_semantic_new.json:
#                the chunked corpus and the semantic merge of what was already
#                submitted. Re-ingesting graphify-out/ re-sends that payload, so
#                this is the most load-bearing line in this file.
# spike-out/     Recorded classifier cassettes rather than source. corpus.json
#                and jev-cassette.json key cached answers by shell commands that
#                read Obsidian vault note paths, e.g.
#                books/kenapa-kita-gampang-percaya-hoax/src/content/11_*.md.
#                That is another corpus's personal content, cached here.
# bun.lock       Resolved dependency metadata for third-party packages: the
#                same class as node_modules/ in GROUP 2, in a single file.
# .git/          Object store and refs.
# node_modules/  Vendored third-party packages, 556 MB in this working copy.
graphify-out/
spike-out/
bun.lock
.git/
node_modules/
